WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.460 --> 00:00:07.600
High Speed Rail lines began popping up in
Europe and Asia in the early ‘80s.

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:09.560
And passengers were exhilarated.

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:12.260
Trains are, for lack of a better word, sexy.

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:18.500
Going at two hundred miles per hour on land
sounds very exciting, very futuristic.

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:21.780
Politicians see opportunity for a legacy for
themselves.

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:26.340
With high-profile rollouts in France and Japan,
bullet train mania was underway.

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:31.960
And then reality set in.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:38.840
Unfortunately, the cost of building such projects
usually vastly outweighed the benefits.

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:42.620
Supporters, who claim that most high speed
rail systems operate at a profit,

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:46.820
use accounting tricks like leaving out construction
costs and indirect subsidies.

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:48.520
If you tabulate real costs,

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:52.280
Two high speed rail lines in the world operate
at a profit.

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:55.340
Rail is more of a 19th century technology.

00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:58.030
We don't have to go through these headaches
and cost overruns

00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:01.360
to build a future transportation system.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:04.960
What we're talking about is a vision for high
speed rail in America.

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:09.600
But politicians can’t resist the ribbon
cutting ceremonies

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:13.040
and imagery of sleek trains hurtling through
the lush countryside.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:14.540
So the projects keep coming.

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:17.940
Don't be afraid of the future.

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:21.530
California’s high speed rail line was sold
to voters on the bold promise

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:25.260
that it will someday whisk passengers between
San Francisco and Los Angeles

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:26.820
in under 3 hours.

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:29.810
Nine years later, the project has turned into
such a disaster

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:33.840
that its biggest political champion is now
suing to stop it from happening.

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:40.360
I say we cannot afford not to pass Proposition
1A

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:45.280
and build high speed rail in California.

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:58.020
It is foolish and it is almost a crime to
sell bonds and encumber the taxpayers of California

00:01:58.030 --> 00:02:02.580
at a time when this is no longer high speed
rail.

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:05.860
An icon of California politics known as the
“Great Dissenter,”

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:09.200
Quentin Kopp introduced the legislation that
established the rail line,

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:11.760
and became chairman of the High-Speed Rail
Authority.

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:17.780
The litigation which is pending will result,
I am confident,

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:24.340
in the termination of the High Speed Rail
Authority's deceiving plan.

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:27.040
Voters supported the bond measure to pay for
construction

00:02:27.049 --> 00:02:29.689
on the condition that the train would be self-sustaining.

00:02:29.689 --> 00:02:32.640
According to one recent estimate, the project’s
latest iteration

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:36.219
would suck up at least $100 million in annual
subsidies.

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:40.560
The ballot measure prohibits taxpayer subsidy

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:48.700
and that was an important part of convincing
voters in 2008 to approve the bond measure.

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:52.340
In the meantime, lawsuits have multiplied,
private investors have fled,

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:58.240
and even the official price tag has nearly
doubled, from $33 billion to $64 billion.

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:00.591
When the legislature cleared the way for the
Rail Authority

00:03:00.591 --> 00:03:04.180
to begin selling the voter-approved bonds
in early 2017,

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:06.510
the agency declared it a “milestone.”

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:07.999
Kopp was livid.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:13.460
It's deceit, that's not a milestone, it's
desperation.

00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:17.940
Because High Speed Rail Authority is out of
money.

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:19.960
You've got to do what you promised the voters.

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:22.980
You can't change that without going back to
the voters.

00:03:22.989 --> 00:03:27.049
Attorney Stuart Flashman has represented environmental and transportation groups

00:03:27.049 --> 00:03:29.599
in several lawsuits against the Rail Authority.

00:03:29.599 --> 00:03:31.620
He now aims to stop the project on the grounds

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:34.650
that the agency broke numerous promises to
voters.

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:37.269
They're going the wrong way.

00:03:37.269 --> 00:03:41.919
They're basically doing this in a way that
is very inefficient and will not work.

00:03:41.919 --> 00:03:44.579
Baruch Feigenbaum says that starting construction

00:03:44.579 --> 00:03:47.579
even though there isn’t enough money allocated
to finish the project

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:51.580
is part of a deliberate plan to extract further
taxpayer subsidies.

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:54.180
Their strategy is to get enough of it built

00:03:54.189 --> 00:03:57.580
so that basically there's going to be so much
money sunk in the project

00:03:57.580 --> 00:04:00.040
that they're gonna argue it's gonna be cheaper
to complete it

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:02.540
than it's going to be to abandon it.

00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:05.780
Kopp and Flashman, however, still believe
in high speed rail.

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:09.700
It’s just that this particular train has
been hijacked by special interests.

00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:13.520
Right now it's a boondoggle, I have to agree
with that, and it's sad.

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:15.200
And why couldn't they do it right?

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:19.780
Because they made a bunch of political promises
to people along the way

00:04:19.780 --> 00:04:22.310
'we'll go through your city' 'oh and we'll
go through your city'

00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:29.180
It's winding it's way around, adding something
like 70 miles beyond the most direct route.

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:32.520
Since rail projects are driven more by politics
than consumer demand,

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:34.820
nonsensical design decisions are typical.

00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:36.660
That’s true even in France and Japan,

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:40.039
where a couple of the first high speed rail
lines were actually profitable.

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:44.600
After building those lines, both of those
countries built a bunch of other lines

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:46.940
that have no hope of ever being profitable.

00:04:46.940 --> 00:04:50.380
Not because the rail folks necessarily wanted
to build them,

00:04:50.389 --> 00:04:55.139
but because the politicians said 'hey city
A has rail, I want it in my city.'

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:58.580
Flashman says the California project has also
become a land grab.

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:01.120
This was going to go right through the middle
of Kings Country,

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:02.800
right across people's farmland.

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:07.240
And we had a farmer whose land it was going
to cross, fellow named John Tos,

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:08.930
and his name is on the lawsuit.

00:05:08.930 --> 00:05:12.020
By design, high-speed rail lines require wide
swaths of land,

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:14.180
which often means seizing property.

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:16.760
Even getting rural land can be a problem,

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:21.020
there's a project in Texas that is proposing
to build a high speed rail line

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:22.060
between Dallas and Houston,

00:05:22.060 --> 00:05:24.140
and one of the reasons why it may not be viable

00:05:24.150 --> 00:05:28.240
is the large amount of rural land they have
to seize from ranchers.

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:31.310
Ranchers have had this land in their family
for generations,

00:05:31.310 --> 00:05:36.940
they might be growing crops on it and they're
not real interested in selling that land.

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:42.880
As for the often-promised environmental benefits
of high-speed rail,

00:05:42.889 --> 00:05:45.900
Flashman acknowledges they won’t materialize
in California.

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:51.500
Ironically doing this high speed rail construction
with huge amounts of concrete and steel involved,

00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:54.380
is actually increasing greenhouse gas production.

00:05:54.380 --> 00:05:57.599
It would probably take 50 to 80 years

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:02.920
in order to negate all of the greenhouse gasses
emitted during the building of the line.

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:06.860
California’s project is extraordinary in
some ways.

00:06:06.860 --> 00:06:11.480
As envisioned, it will be both the slowest
bullet train in the world, and the most expensive.

00:06:11.480 --> 00:06:15.260
They're using a blended track, so it won't
get to the speed they promised,

00:06:15.260 --> 00:06:17.560
because it's going through the central valley

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:21.620
it won't meet the timetable that was laid
out in the ballot initiative.

00:06:21.620 --> 00:06:30.280
It will be conventional rail, which in a way
is parallel to existing Amtrak service.

00:06:30.280 --> 00:06:32.240
It’ll also be competing with air travel

00:06:32.260 --> 00:06:37.420
at a time when a new generation of quiet supersonic
planes is about to take to the skies.

00:06:37.420 --> 00:06:40.479
Autonomous vehicles will soon give passengers
the same freedom

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:43.100
to sleep, work, or read as train travelers.

00:06:43.100 --> 00:06:46.420
And then there’s Elon Musk’s plans for
hyperloop pod transport

00:06:46.430 --> 00:06:50.620
in a near-vacuum tube at speeds up to 800mph.

00:06:50.620 --> 00:06:53.800
I say let Elon Musk develop it.

00:06:53.800 --> 00:07:00.860
'Cause I'm not advocating using taxpayer money
for an unproven, untested concept.

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:11.800
I think if government gets out of the way

00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:16.080
of deciding which transportation modes we need in the future,

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:19.840
the private sector will do a much better job
of innovating

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:24.280
and creating profitable transportation modes that people will want to use

00:07:24.280 --> 00:07:27.720
instead of locking in a sub-optimal choice from the 19th century.

